From 261b2a55325b790ff2998c497ae8e69ac63be0e3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: verkur46 <mohamadrezasahami1999@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 15:19:13 +0330
Subject: [PATCH 011/230] Give the matches feed a relevance window instead of
 oldest-first
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

/api/matches ordered by kickoff_at ASC and capped at 300 rows, so on a
populated database the default feed opened on ancient internationals and
pushed the fixtures the user actually cared about off the end. The old
rows were not even the ones we wanted to evict first — they were all
there was.

Serve a relevance window instead: live and half-time always, finished
matches from the last 3 days, scheduled matches in the next 14, ordered
live → soonest → newest so the sections read top to bottom. Adds optional
from/to (and a date shortcut) for date-range views, and clamps the limit
instead of trusting it.

Matches.tsx now sorts its upcoming and finished sections explicitly rather
than inheriting whatever order the server happened to send, so the client
view does not silently change shape when the query does.

Tests run the generated SQL against a real SQLite file: window boundaries,
ordering, the filters, limit clamping, and the original regression.

